[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced Aggregation Techniques in MongoDB"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"MongoDB's aggregation framework provides powerful tools for data processing and transformation. This tutorial explores advanced aggregation techniques, focusing on `$lookup` and `$facet`."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"$lookup: Joining Data from Multiple Collections"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `$lookup` stage performs a left outer join to another collection in the same database to filter in documents to join for processing. It adds a new array field to the input documents. The new array field contains the matching documents from the "joined" collection."},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"There are several ways to use `$lookup`, including equality match and using an aggregation pipeline."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Equality Match with $lookup"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"This is the simplest form of `$lookup`. It matches documents based on a single field equality."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"db.orders.aggregate([
  {
    $lookup:
      {
        from: "products",
        localField: "productId",
        foreignField: "_id",
        as: "product"
      }
  }
])"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `orders` collection is joined with the `products` collection where `orders.productId` matches `products._id`. The matching products are added to the `product` field as an array."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"$lookup with an Aggregation Pipeline"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"For more complex join conditions and data transformations, you can use an aggregation pipeline within `$lookup`."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"db.orders.aggregate([
  {
    $lookup: {
      from: "products",
      let: { order_product_id: "$productId", order_quantity: "$quantity" },
      pipeline: [
        { $match: { $expr: { $eq: [ "$_id",  "$$order_product_id" ] } } },
        { $project: { name: 1, price: 1, _id: 0 } }
      ],
      as: "product_info"
    }
  }
])"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, `let` defines variables accessible within the `pipeline`. The `pipeline` performs a `$match` using `$expr` to compare `products._id` with the `order_product_id` variable.  It also uses `$project` to reshape the output from the 'products' collection."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"$facet: Multi-Faceted Aggregation"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `$facet` stage allows you to process a single set of input documents through multiple aggregation pipelines within a single stage. Each sub-pipeline has its own field in the output document."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"db.sales.aggregate([
  {
    $facet: {
      "totalSales": [
        { $group: { _id: null, total: { $sum: "$amount" } } }
      ],
      "salesByProduct": [
        { $group: { _id: "$product", count: { $sum: 1 } } }
      ],
      "averageSale": [
        { $group: { _id: null, average: { $avg: "$amount" } } }
      ]
    }
  }
])"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example calculates total sales, sales by product, and average sale amount in a single aggregation pipeline. Each result is stored in a separate field (`totalSales`, `salesByProduct`, `averageSale`) in the output document."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Combining $lookup and $facet"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can combine `$lookup` and `$facet` for even more complex data analysis. For example, you can use `$lookup` to join data and then `$facet` to perform different aggregations on the joined data."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"db.orders.aggregate([
  {
    $lookup: {
      from: "products",
      localField: "productId",
      foreignField: "_id",
      as: "product"
    }
  },
  {
    $unwind: "$product"
  },
  {
    $facet: {
      "totalOrders": [
        { $count: "count" }
      ],
      "averagePrice": [
        { $group: { _id: null, average: { $avg: "$product.price" } } }
      ]
    }
  }
])"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"This pipeline first joins `orders` with `products` using `$lookup`. Then, `$unwind` is used to deconstruct the 'product' array. Finally, `$facet` calculates the total number of orders and the average product price."},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"These advanced aggregation techniques provide powerful tools for data analysis and transformation in MongoDB. Understanding and utilizing `$lookup` and `$facet` can significantly improve your ability to extract valuable insights from your data."}]
